<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Duckmetry.Core\Duckmetry.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Release versioning. The project file itself is named Duckmetry.Cli.csproj, so
    MSBuild would otherwise emit Duckmetry.Cli.exe. Set Version below and override
    AssemblyName to Duckmetry.Cli.<version> so each published exe identifies itself:
        Duckmetry.Cli.2.16.0.exe
    Release scripts pass /p:Version=<tag> to keep the two in step.
  -->

  <!--
    Publish settings, kept here rather than on the command line so that every publish
    produces the same shape by default.

    IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract is REQUIRED. A single-file publish cannot embed
    a native library by default: duckdb.dll (36 MB, loaded by DuckDB.NET at runtime) is
    written BESIDE the exe instead, and the exe alone does not work without it.

    That is exactly how v2.16.0 shipped. The release carried one asset, the .exe, so every
    conversion failed identically:

        [Error] Conversion failed for <file>: Object reference not set to an instance of
        an object.

    Reproduced and confirmed: with duckdb.dll beside the exe the same build converts
    normally; with the exe alone it throws
        System.NullReferenceException
           at DuckDB.NET.Data.DuckDBConnectionStringBuilder.set_Item(String, Object)
           at System.Data.Common.DbConnectionStringBuilder.set_ConnectionString(String)
           at DuckDB.NET.Data.DuckDBConnection.Open()
    The managed wrapper dereferences a field its own static initialiser left null because
    the native library was not there to initialise it, so the null surfaces four frames
    away from the real cause - which is why it read as a mystery rather than a packaging
    fault. v2.15.0 was 108 MB because it shipped as a folder with duckdb.dll beside it.

    With this set, the single exe is self-sufficient: 108,543,350 bytes, no siblings.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>1.0.1</Version>
    <AssemblyName>Duckmetry.Cli.$(Version)</AssemblyName>
    <!-- The exe's icon in Explorer/taskbar (Windows publishes only). -->
    <ApplicationIcon>..\..\assets\duckmetry.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!--
      Windows shows these in the file's Properties dialog and in the task manager. They
      are the first thing a user sees when something goes wrong, so they have to state
      what the tool is without implying it belongs to either company whose formats it
      reads. The banner and the About box carry the full non-affiliation notice.
    -->
    <Product>Duckmetry - unofficial community converter</Product>
    <Company>Independent community project</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 jay-slt. Not affiliated with MoTeC Ltd or Studio 397.</Copyright>
    <Description>Converts locally recorded DuckDB telemetry into i2-readable .ld/.ldx files. Independent tool; not endorsed by MoTeC Ltd or Studio 397.</Description>
    <FileVersion>$(Version)</FileVersion>
    <AssemblyVersion>$(Version)</AssemblyVersion>
    <InformationalVersion>$(Version)</InformationalVersion>

    <!-- Publish shape. See the note above: without IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract
         the single exe is missing duckdb.dll and every conversion fails. -->
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>